diagnosis_text:
A complete four-phase investigation found no network fault anywhere in the simple_bgp topology. Phase 1 reachability showed 0% packet loss and a round-trip time of approximately 1.25 ms in both directions between pc1 (195.11.14.2) and pc2 (200.1.1.2) — well within normal virtual-lab baseline. Phase A infrastructure sweep found no ACL or firewall drop rules, no static ARP poisoning, no IP or gateway misconfiguration, and no duplicate MAC addresses on any of the four devices. Phase B confirmed the BGP control plane is fully healthy: both router1 (ASN 1) and router2 (ASN 2) have all FRR daemons active (watchfrr, zebra, bgpd), a single BGP session in the Established state, and their respective prefixes (195.11.14.0/24 and 200.1.1.0/24) correctly advertised. The tc_snapshot found no traffic-control shaping on any of the six inspected interfaces — all show only the Linux default fq_codel qdisc, which is a latency-reducing discipline, not a bandwidth limiter or delay injector. Phase D pressure_sweep found no stress or flood tool running on any device and no CPU or socket pressure. The user's claim that inter-AS peering is "congested and adding huge latency" is directly contradicted by live measurements: there is no tbf rate-limiter, no netem delay, no resource overload, and no routing anomaly. The perceived slowness is not attributable to any network fault present in the topology at the time of investigation.
=== END ===